1
00:02:07,940 --> 00:02:09,150
 Because no one has gone there.

2
00:02:10,160 --> 00:02:12,420
 Many explorers have tried, but they all died in the ice.

3
00:02:12,960 --> 00:02:14,880
 Could I be the first one to get there?

4
00:02:15,090 --> 00:02:17,460
 First one to reach the North Pole?

5
00:02:17,600 --> 00:02:18,780
 Son, it's pretty unlikely.

6
00:02:18,980 --> 00:02:20,880
 Several expeditions are underway now.

7
00:02:21,060 --> 00:02:22,020
 The others are getting ready.

8
00:02:23,310 --> 00:02:25,460
 Like that. Born too late, that's all.

9
00:02:33,990 --> 00:02:39,910
 I do. I guess I better get used to the coal.

10
00:02:41,560 --> 00:02:43,610
 I go all the way to the pole.

11
00:02:43,810 --> 00:02:45,290
 You'd risk your life for nothing.

12
00:02:45,410 --> 00:02:46,910
 The unknown is always a challenge.

13
00:02:47,630 --> 00:02:50,130
 A man wants to explore and discover what new lands are like.

14
00:02:50,130 --> 00:02:53,250
 No matter how difficult or bleak, anyway, the poles are magical.

15
00:02:55,520 --> 00:02:58,370
 Remember, the Earth revolves on an axis passing through its poles.

16
00:02:58,410 --> 00:03:00,970
 When at the North Pole, the cardinal points no longer exist.

17
00:03:01,050 --> 00:03:02,230
 Now what? Now what points?

18
00:03:02,570 --> 00:03:04,450
 Cardinal points, know that.

19
00:03:04,830 --> 00:03:06,530
 If you're facing north and to your right is east,

20
00:03:06,590 --> 00:03:08,030
 and on your left is the west and...

21
00:03:08,030 --> 00:03:09,730
 Absolutely right.

22
00:03:10,080 --> 00:03:11,960
 But suppose you're up at the North Pole.

23
00:03:13,540 --> 00:03:17,560
 Left, directions, or south?

24
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:19,170
 East, you never lost.

25
00:03:19,500 --> 00:03:21,610
 Point in any direction, still south.

26
00:03:21,920 --> 00:03:23,160
 Feel like south going left.

27
00:03:23,390 --> 00:03:24,540
 Go to the right, it's south.

28
00:03:24,820 --> 00:03:26,500
 In front, behind, south, out.

29
00:03:26,520 --> 00:03:28,820
 Yeah, but there's another reason motivating explorers

30
00:03:28,820 --> 00:03:29,900
 to know what's at the poles.

31
00:03:30,060 --> 00:03:32,260
 Finding the northeast and northwest passages,

32
00:03:32,580 --> 00:03:34,480
 the shortest sea routes between Europe and Asia,

33
00:03:34,480 --> 00:03:35,940
 and between Europe and the Americas.

34
00:03:36,520 --> 00:03:37,800
 But are they open always?

35
00:03:37,940 --> 00:03:40,860
 You remember Bering had tried to answer that question.

36
00:03:41,750 --> 00:03:42,430
 Father!

37
00:03:42,610 --> 00:03:43,570
 What is it, Roald?

38
00:03:43,710 --> 00:03:48,320
 The bridge of Nansen crossed the Greenland Ice Cap.

39
00:03:48,480 --> 00:03:50,780
 Now he's looking for a sea route to the North Pole, Dad!

40
00:03:50,840 --> 00:03:53,160
 You're like a compass. You always point north.

41
00:03:53,620 --> 00:03:57,240
 1897, the first attempt to explore Antarctica, the South Pole.

42
00:03:57,240 --> 00:03:59,740
 The captain is a Belgian, Adrian de Gerlach.

43
00:04:00,180 --> 00:04:02,940
 His second-in-command, the Norwegian, Roald Amundsen.

44
00:04:03,360 --> 00:04:06,460
 He's 25, and it'll be his first voyage to the polar region,

45
00:04:06,620 --> 00:04:08,260
 Exactly opposite to the pole in the north.

46
00:04:09,490 --> 00:04:15,300
 The ice is closing in.

47
00:04:15,580 --> 00:04:16,780
 We should sail for home.

48
00:04:16,980 --> 00:04:19,550
 Yes, I believe you're right, Amos.

49
00:04:19,810 --> 00:04:21,640
 Take the channel, it's free!

50
00:04:28,440 --> 00:04:36,110
 It's bound here all winter.

51
00:04:36,430 --> 00:04:38,830
 But we've no warm clothes, and our supplies are short.

52
00:04:38,990 --> 00:04:40,230
 We'll just have to manage.

53
00:04:40,490 --> 00:04:41,980
 And quickly before the polar night.

54
00:04:47,090 --> 00:04:52,510
 Captain, mad as a hatter, look.

55
00:04:55,370 --> 00:05:04,860
 Doctor, take command of the ship.

56
00:05:04,960 --> 00:05:06,080
 Now go and get that man.

57
00:05:06,080 --> 00:05:08,880
 We'll cut our bed covers and make ourselves warm clothing.

58
00:05:20,260 --> 00:05:20,780
 It's for fuel.

59
00:05:21,610 --> 00:05:24,480
 We'll burn fat, Doctor, the fat of seals and penguins.

60
00:05:37,080 --> 00:05:43,000
 Begins now.

61
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:53,570
 There isn't going to be any sun for three months.

62
00:05:53,570 --> 00:06:05,010
 Time Explorer has survived the glacial winter.

63
00:06:05,010 --> 00:06:11,370
 For Amundsen, it was a springboard to further exploits.

64
00:06:11,370 --> 00:06:16,180
 There, Helmer, a small fishing boat for sale, and the price is right.

65
00:06:16,180 --> 00:06:16,560
 Chance the light.

66
00:06:16,560 --> 00:06:18,530
 What would you do with it, Raul?

67
00:06:18,530 --> 00:06:20,860
 I'll find the Northwest Passage.

68
00:06:20,860 --> 00:06:23,440
 In that nutshell, you're crazy.

69
00:06:23,440 --> 00:06:24,600
 Include me out, well.

70
00:06:24,600 --> 00:06:26,300
 Too bad, I'll see about supplies.

71
00:06:32,740 --> 00:06:37,630
 today sir and what is it I'll tell you I need supplies for an expedition

72
00:06:37,630 --> 00:06:44,630
 everything we'll need yes and how many will you be six or seven and I'd say

73
00:06:44,630 --> 00:06:49,270
 five years long that'll be quite an order I want everything delivered to

74
00:06:49,270 --> 00:06:54,240
 captain Amundsen in the port I'll see to it sir a very good day to you sir

75
00:06:54,240 --> 00:07:10,320
 that I had 22 times captain Amundsen I've put in 22 boxes of tried biscuits a

76
00:07:10,320 --> 00:07:16,200
 A quantity of dried beef, 10 cod liver oil, 20 barrels of kerosene, 20 barrels of flowers.

77
00:07:16,340 --> 00:07:19,420
 That's fine. Figure it exactly. Then put it all on my account.

78
00:07:19,680 --> 00:07:21,220
 I'll pay you just as soon as I return.

79
00:07:21,540 --> 00:07:22,260
 When you return!

80
00:07:22,540 --> 00:07:24,480
 In this leaky bucket, you'll never come back.

81
00:07:24,600 --> 00:07:27,850
 Oh, no, no, no, no. You'll pay me here and now, and I have no credit.

82
00:07:27,950 --> 00:07:29,850
 Otherwise, I'll stand there.

83
00:07:30,890 --> 00:07:33,790
 You've no reason to worry. You'll get paid.

84
00:07:34,010 --> 00:07:37,600
 But now I'm busy. They're my dogs. Huskies. We'll need them, all right?

85
00:07:37,740 --> 00:07:39,060
 Good. Bring them aboard.

86
00:07:39,650 --> 00:07:41,130
 Helmer, what are you doing here?

87
00:07:41,550 --> 00:07:42,830
 Oh, just out for a walk.

88
00:07:42,830 --> 00:07:44,170
 They're splendid dogs.

89
00:07:44,310 --> 00:07:46,970
 They're used by Eskimos. You know all about them.

90
00:07:46,970 --> 00:07:50,070
 And if you come, you're in charge of them. You can train them.

91
00:07:50,070 --> 00:07:53,170
 We sail in that nutshell never.

92
00:07:53,170 --> 00:08:07,050
 On a trip? Oh, get him!

93
00:08:07,050 --> 00:08:09,260
 Captain, police are coming!

94
00:08:09,260 --> 00:08:11,680
 Oh, I have more important matters!

95
00:08:11,680 --> 00:08:14,990
 Stand by the cast-off! We're underway! Come on, Hellboy!

96
00:08:14,990 --> 00:08:16,220
 Wait a minute, all right.

97
00:08:16,220 --> 00:08:58,880
 This is beginning to violent.

98
00:08:58,880 --> 00:09:48,220
 Eskimos are friendly.

99
00:09:48,220 --> 00:10:01,630
 What? Speaking English?

100
00:10:01,630 --> 00:10:05,930
 Yeah, yeah, yeah. Long time ago, when I was a young man, some Englishman came here.

101
00:10:05,930 --> 00:10:07,470
 So I speak it.

102
00:10:07,470 --> 00:10:08,570
 What happened to them?

103
00:10:08,570 --> 00:10:10,470
 Oh, that is me, dogs.

104
00:10:10,470 --> 00:10:12,010
 What, because they didn't have dogs?

105
00:10:12,010 --> 00:10:14,430
 Yeah, here in the far north, you need dogs to survive!

106
00:10:14,430 --> 00:10:16,200
 I have six of them.

107
00:10:16,200 --> 00:10:17,120
 Alright, let's see him.

108
00:10:17,120 --> 00:10:18,530
 I think he's our friend.

109
00:10:18,530 --> 00:10:19,810
 Go and get our dogs.

110
00:10:19,810 --> 00:10:27,980
 Mmm.

111
00:10:27,980 --> 00:10:35,150
 Oh, trained yet.

112
00:10:35,150 --> 00:10:35,960
 See that?

113
00:10:35,960 --> 00:10:37,050
 Yeah, we can help train him.

114
00:10:37,050 --> 00:10:41,760
 Use the whip with a great deal of precision.

115
00:10:41,760 --> 00:10:43,900
 Dog barely gets touched.

116
00:10:43,900 --> 00:10:51,200
 Damn, precision with the whip!

117
00:10:51,200 --> 00:10:52,710
 No, it takes great skill to be...

118
00:10:52,710 --> 00:10:54,320
 ...sup.

119
00:10:54,320 --> 00:11:06,110
 And your dogs, you gotta feed 'em.

120
00:11:06,110 --> 00:11:09,280
 Nice big chunks of seal, fresh bear meat, that's best.

121
00:11:09,280 --> 00:11:12,320
 Here are boys, Dindins.

122
00:11:12,320 --> 00:11:13,370
 Heh heh, oh.

123
00:11:13,370 --> 00:11:15,050
 Guess they like him.

124
00:11:15,050 --> 00:11:20,450
 I should explain. With working dogs, there's a boss dog and a lieutenant.

125
00:11:20,450 --> 00:11:22,010
 You gotta respect the rank.

126
00:11:22,010 --> 00:11:23,140
 The rank?

127
00:11:23,140 --> 00:11:24,880
 Yeah, but he's not gonna say.

128
00:11:24,880 --> 00:11:29,860
 He is the boss, and he's next.

129
00:11:29,860 --> 00:11:31,640
 And he's the next in line.

130
00:11:31,640 --> 00:11:36,250
 They're wild, but disciplined.

131
00:11:36,250 --> 00:11:42,590
 Your clothes are too tight.

132
00:11:42,590 --> 00:11:45,470
 Make skins and furs to make other ones for you.

133
00:11:45,470 --> 00:11:49,570
 We'll begin with the big one. You will stand up.

134
00:11:49,570 --> 00:11:52,160
 Ouch.

135
00:11:52,160 --> 00:11:53,790
 Many skins to cover him.

136
00:11:53,790 --> 00:11:57,050
 We can hunt bear now that daylight has come.

137
00:11:57,050 --> 00:11:59,010
 The bear is tasty.

138
00:11:59,010 --> 00:12:42,800
 Years, and then, one day...

139
00:12:42,800 --> 00:12:48,670
 Use axes.

140
00:12:48,670 --> 00:12:59,970
 And grappling hooks.

141
00:12:59,970 --> 00:13:43,480
 ...sailed around the North Pole going east and west, but no one has yet reached the pole itself,

142
00:13:43,580 --> 00:13:46,080
 and that turns into a real race to get there first.

143
00:13:46,570 --> 00:13:51,950
 In 1895, the Norwegian Nansen has to abandon his ship trapped in the ice just 450 kilometers from his goal,

144
00:13:52,030 --> 00:13:52,970
 and he goes on by kayak.

145
00:13:53,550 --> 00:13:59,270
 In 1896, the Swedish explorer Solomon André made another attempt in a hot air balloon and died on the way.

146
00:13:59,650 --> 00:14:04,350
 In 1890, Louis Amadei of Savoy came to within 400 kilometers of the pole and disappeared.

147
00:14:04,780 --> 00:14:08,760
 The American Admiral Robert Perry organized eight successive expeditions.

148
00:14:08,760 --> 00:14:11,180
 You may be sure Amundsen was in the race.

149
00:14:15,630 --> 00:14:18,620
 Mr. Amundsen, this is a surprise.

150
00:14:19,270 --> 00:14:20,980
 It is an honor to serve you, Captain.

151
00:14:21,140 --> 00:14:23,860
 As I recall last time, you sent the police after us, sir.

152
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:25,440
 I was young and impetuous.

153
00:14:25,700 --> 00:14:26,540
 What can I say?

154
00:14:26,990 --> 00:14:29,300
 Yes, well, now I'm here to settle the debt.

155
00:14:29,780 --> 00:14:30,620
 Roll square.

156
00:14:30,880 --> 00:14:38,250
 Well, that is, I believe that you've forgotten my compound interest, sir.

157
00:14:38,760 --> 00:14:39,710
 Interest on interest?

158
00:14:40,730 --> 00:14:41,590
 Compound interest.

159
00:14:41,590 --> 00:14:44,990
 But, Mr. Amundsen, it's been such a long time, and time is money.

160
00:14:45,190 --> 00:14:48,770
 Oh!

161
00:14:48,770 --> 00:14:53,430
 For your next expedition, I am ready to serve you, sir.

162
00:14:53,430 --> 00:14:56,430
 Biscuits, dried meat, salted carrots, sugar flour, anything at all!

163
00:14:56,430 --> 00:14:59,190
 Expedition, we've had enough.

164
00:14:59,190 --> 00:15:01,250
 We've no intention to go on more expeditions.

165
00:15:01,250 --> 00:15:03,340
 I mean, you've discovered the Northwest Passage.

166
00:15:03,340 --> 00:15:07,260
 No one's reached the Pole yet, and who knows, I could be the first.

167
00:15:07,260 --> 00:15:10,530
 But Robert Perry's on his way, and Frederick Scott as well.

168
00:15:10,530 --> 00:15:13,530
 The Pole, you realize, six months of darkness, maybe you'll freeze.

169
00:15:13,530 --> 00:15:15,530
 No, sir, not me. Oh, no, may heaven fall.

170
00:15:15,530 --> 00:15:17,530
 Oh, I say that's a good omen.

171
00:15:17,530 --> 00:15:25,730
 The fram, another leaky old tub. It's all rotten. Somebody ought to chop it up and burn it from firewood.

172
00:15:25,730 --> 00:15:26,730
 Come and take a look. Look at what.

173
00:15:26,730 --> 00:15:36,600
 I'll show you. It's built to withstand Arctic ice. The hull can be reinforced, and I'll put in a diesel engine.

174
00:15:36,600 --> 00:15:42,600
 Never! You understand? I am never taking this through Arctic ice. May heaven, uh, fall on my head.

175
00:15:42,600 --> 00:15:50,370
 You see, I'll go up the coast of Greenland. I'll be icebound in winter, and the ice flows north.

176
00:15:50,370 --> 00:15:55,760
 May heaven fall...

177
00:15:55,760 --> 00:15:56,220
 The pole?

178
00:15:56,480 --> 00:15:57,580
 Homer, it's not heaven.

179
00:15:57,720 --> 00:15:58,120
 A bulb.

180
00:15:58,260 --> 00:15:58,720
 That's all.

181
00:15:58,980 --> 00:16:00,260
 All right, Roald.

182
00:16:00,380 --> 00:16:01,890
 I'd like to manage the huskies.

183
00:16:02,380 --> 00:16:03,930
 If you like, I'll go part of the way.

184
00:16:04,390 --> 00:16:04,860
 You understand?

185
00:16:05,100 --> 00:16:06,000
 Sure, sure, I understand.

186
00:16:06,340 --> 00:16:07,940
 In one month, everything will be ready.

187
00:16:13,200 --> 00:16:17,800
 It was for you, Mr. Amundsen.

188
00:16:17,990 --> 00:16:19,410
 The North Pole has been conquered.

189
00:16:20,740 --> 00:16:21,220
 It's what?

190
00:16:21,710 --> 00:16:29,400
 Hey, period.

191
00:16:33,510 --> 00:16:36,100
 Wanted to be the first one.

192
00:16:38,380 --> 00:16:41,070
 Of course.

193
00:16:41,400 --> 00:16:42,330
 It's the other challenge.

194
00:16:45,040 --> 00:16:48,160
 You said you'd supply us on our next expedition.

195
00:16:48,510 --> 00:16:49,910
 We're going to the North Pole.

196
00:16:50,560 --> 00:16:51,240
 We'll need all this.

197
00:16:51,240 --> 00:16:55,180
 Biscuits, fuel oil, dried meat, flour, cooking oil, paraffin, oil for lamps, explosives,

198
00:16:55,400 --> 00:17:02,800
 Weapons, ammunition, six dog sleds, 25 pair of skis, one prefabricated house, five pigs, six sheep, a cat to chase rats, and what was the last thing?

199
00:17:02,960 --> 00:17:04,900
 Oh, yes, dogs. Where are the huskies? Huh?

200
00:17:05,370 --> 00:17:10,250
 Right, Helmer, how many have you got? A hundred?

201
00:17:10,490 --> 00:17:12,250
 For the North Pole, they're the best you can get.

202
00:17:12,550 --> 00:17:13,170
 Perfect.

203
00:17:13,740 --> 00:17:15,660
 We are going to the North Pole, aren't we?

204
00:17:15,660 --> 00:17:17,240
 Yes, of course. Where else would we go?

205
00:17:19,510 --> 00:17:24,650
 Peterson said he was going to sail through the Bering Strait to the west of Alaska.

206
00:17:24,650 --> 00:17:29,430
 He said it was going to save time since his earlier voyage seemed interminable to him.

207
00:17:29,960 --> 00:17:36,340
 He planned a course to the southwest right here, past South America, and then back up to the Pacific, heading toward the north.

208
00:17:36,840 --> 00:17:38,660
 And presto, here he is on the equator.

209
00:17:40,930 --> 00:17:45,500
 I'm on deck. Gotta see this.

210
00:17:52,600 --> 00:18:07,020
 First time across, you're initiated.

211
00:18:08,580 --> 00:18:12,040
 To Neptune, Sudden Hemisphere.

212
00:18:13,020 --> 00:18:17,330
 I'd better tell you, I've got another little surprise.

213
00:18:18,520 --> 00:18:20,290
 Oh yeah? What's your surprise?

214
00:18:20,550 --> 00:18:21,850
 We're not going to the North Pole.

215
00:18:21,890 --> 00:18:22,330
 What?

216
00:18:22,530 --> 00:18:28,160
 We're going to the South Pole. Robert Perry's been to the North Pole, right? We'll be the first ones to get to the South Pole.

217
00:18:28,280 --> 00:18:31,660
 But Robert Scott, a British officer, is already on his way.

218
00:18:31,740 --> 00:18:34,000
 Then we'd better hurry. The race is on.

219
00:18:34,420 --> 00:18:36,480
 And Amundsen headed for Whale Bay.

220
00:18:40,940 --> 00:19:15,820
 Already on the ice.

221
00:19:31,360 --> 00:19:36,320
 And Amundsen are about 1,400 kilometers away now.

222
00:19:36,460 --> 00:19:38,360
 In summer, Amundsen chose his best husky.

223
00:19:38,480 --> 00:19:42,460
 Scott is using Manchurian ponies, used to the cold, and a motorized sleigh.

224
00:19:42,820 --> 00:19:44,440
 Yeah, but which one is going to win?

225
00:19:44,440 --> 00:19:54,320
 In April, the polar night begins.

226
00:19:54,970 --> 00:19:57,570
 We have two months to put supplies along our route to the pole.

227
00:19:57,670 --> 00:19:58,670
 We'd better begin now.

228
00:20:09,820 --> 00:20:17,730
 Turn now.

229
00:20:56,470 --> 00:20:57,870
 Sun's going below the horizon.

230
00:20:57,980 --> 00:21:04,860
 We aren't going to see it again for four months.

231
00:21:04,860 --> 00:21:12,350
 It's fine.

232
00:21:12,350 --> 00:21:15,260
 Red tents are more visible, and could be warmer.

233
00:21:15,260 --> 00:21:27,810
 Ha ha!

234
00:21:27,810 --> 00:21:28,890
 It's hard to teach you!

235
00:21:28,890 --> 00:21:29,530
 Oh! Oh!

236
00:21:29,530 --> 00:21:34,430
 We've lived...

237
00:21:34,430 --> 00:21:36,640
 ...was months long. Today we'll see the sun.

238
00:21:36,640 --> 00:21:50,110
 Sunny day was pretty short.

239
00:21:50,110 --> 00:21:54,010
 We've waited six months. Now let's go.

240
00:21:54,010 --> 00:21:59,280
 Mark!

241
00:21:59,280 --> 00:22:17,330
 There's 6,000 meters in altitude.

242
00:22:17,330 --> 00:22:18,930
 We have to find a passage.

243
00:22:18,930 --> 00:22:26,540
 That way.

244
00:22:26,540 --> 00:22:33,890
 Over there.

245
00:22:33,890 --> 00:22:42,210
 He's a sweat and frozen.

246
00:22:42,210 --> 00:22:43,590
 Now they can't move.

247
00:22:43,590 --> 00:22:44,320
 Destroy them.

248
00:22:44,320 --> 00:23:55,610
 Sound.

249
00:23:55,610 --> 00:23:57,690
 Two hundred and forty leagues.

250
00:23:57,690 --> 00:24:05,290
 Straight ahead.

251
00:24:05,290 --> 00:24:15,420
 Nine degrees, thirty-five minutes.

252
00:24:15,500 --> 00:24:16,570
 We're almost there.

253
00:24:16,570 --> 00:24:37,670
 It's ironic. All I'd ever thought of was the North Pole.

254
00:24:37,670 --> 00:24:39,330
 Now I'm as far away as I can be.

255
00:24:39,330 --> 00:24:46,060
 Great!

256
00:24:46,060 --> 00:24:50,450
 Flag, they beat us to it.

257
00:24:50,450 --> 00:24:57,920
 Let's take the picture, then we'll return.

258
00:24:57,920 --> 00:25:05,810
 Captain Scott and his men arrived at the South Pole one month after Amundsen,

259
00:25:05,810 --> 00:25:08,540
 but like so many other explorers before them,

260
00:25:08,540 --> 00:25:12,890
 who had bravely faced polar ice and snow and piercing glacial winds,

261
00:25:12,890 --> 00:25:14,870
 none of these men returned.

262
00:25:14,870 --> 00:25:19,890
 Today at the South Pole, there is in their honor a base named Amundsen Scott.

